  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 1217 AM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nForecast expectations remain roughly the same tonight as the past \nseveral nights, with warm and quiet weather persisting through \nSaturday, with a passing cold front on Sunday bringing showers and a \nsharp cooldown for the second half of the forecast period.\n\nAn expansive and strong dome of surface high pressure will remain in \ncontrol across the area through the end of the work week, with the \nperiphery of the high remaining the primary influence even into \nSaturday.\n\nSkies will be clear to partly cloudy through Saturday, with \ncontinued well above normal temperatures, possibly threatening \nrecord highs on Saturday. Lows will be above normal but still crisp \nat times with nearly ideal radiational cooling conditions expected \nseveral nights. Some patchy fog concern may need watching on several \nnights, particularly in stream valleys and other climatologically \nfavored areas, but with ample sun on many days, along with over 90 \npercent of the state at least abnormally dry and moderate drought \nongoing across a third of Indiana, widespread fog concerns should be \nminimal.\n\nChange comes again Sunday as an elongated shortwave passes through \nthe region. Associated with this shortwave looks to be a moderate \nsurface low north of the Great Lakes with a lengthy cold front but a \nrelatively narrow warm sector/moist conveyor belt as the surface \nridge's influence back into the Gulf Coast inhibits early moisture \nreturn late in the week. The displacement of the more significant \nforcing northward along with the relatively narrow moist conveyor \nbelt should limit precip coverage and amounts to at least a slight \ndegree, with chances maximized immediately along and ahead of the \nfront, as this will be the primary forcing source in the immediate \nregion, with only weak upright instability expected if any. That \nsaid, despite the limited Gulf moisture return, remnant Pacific \nmoisture from the west coast will be drawn into the system as well, \nhelping to mitigate the aforementioned limitations to some degree, \nand column precipitable water values may at least briefly approach \nor exceed climatological 90th percentile, though rain duration and \ndry antecedent conditions will likely keep this from presenting any \nproblems. Frontal timing has continued to slow slightly and is now \nmore diurnally favorable, but old school K-index values, which have \nutility as a first glance for both surface based and elevated \nconvection, remain unimpressive, and forecast profiles agree with \nlittle to no upright instability depicted.\n\nA return to dry but substantially cooler weather is expected for \nearly next week in the wake of the cold front as another large \nsurface high traverses the Great Lakes region, though this one \nappears to move through a bit more quickly than the one anchored \nover the eastern US this week. Models are beginning to suggest an \nupper level trough, perhaps enhanced a bit by a strong southern \nstream, may allow a return of low precip chances by mid week, but \nuncertainty remains high here, and model disagreement in the \nhandling of this system is substantial. Temperatures are likely to \nbe 20 or so degrees colder by next Monday than they were to start \nthe weekend, with much cooler conditions continuing through the \nThanksgiving holiday week.",
